        "chapter": "Chromatography",
        "chapterNumber": 19,
        "topic": "Chromatography",
        "question": "What is the mathematical formula for calculating the Retention Factor ($R_f$ value) of a separated chemical solute spot in paper chromatography?",
        "options": {
          "A": "$R_f = \\frac{\\text{Distance traveled by solute component from baseline}}{\\text{Distance traveled by solvent front from baseline}}$",
          "B": "$R_f = \\frac{\\text{Distance traveled by solvent front}}{\\text{Distance traveled by solute}}$",
          "C": "$R_f = \\text{Distance of solute} \\times \\text{Distance of solvent}$",
          "D": "$R_f = \\frac{\\text{Solvent density}}{\\text{Solute molecular mass}}$"
        },
        "correctAnswer": "A",
        "explanation": "$R_f$ (retention factor) is the dimensionless ratio: $R_f = \\frac{d_{\\text{solute}}}{d_{\\text{solvent}}}$. Since solutes never travel further than the solvent front, $R_f \\le 1.0$.",
        "difficulty": "easy",
        "verified": true,
        "source": "curriculum-bank",
        "createdAt": "2026-08-23T12:00:00.000Z"
